The Moon wept every night for her lost love, her silver tears falling endlessly. But tears from a celestial being cannot simply disappear. Each luminous drop that fell upon her own surface burned into her skin, leaving hollow craters where they landed.

The Sun, seeing his beloved's face becoming marked with sorrow, made a great sacrifice. He gave up some of his brilliant light so that he could occasionally linger in the sky with her during eclipses. During these rare moments, they could be together, and the Moon's tears would stop.

But the damage was done. The Moon's face remained forever marked with the craters of her grief. Yet she wears them proudly, for they remind her that love endures even the greatest distances and the longest nights.

And so when you look up and see the Moon's scarred face, remember: even celestial beings know the pain of longing, and beauty can be found in the marks left by love.

