Long ago, the night sky was empty and dark, causing fear among the people. They huddled by their fires, afraid of the endless void above.

In a small village lived an old weaver named Aria, known for creating the most beautiful tapestries. One evening, the Moon Goddess appeared before her, lamenting the sky's emptiness.

"The darkness frightens my children below," the goddess said. "Can you help me?"

Aria thought deeply, then gathered silver threads from her finest work. All night she wove, her fingers dancing across an invisible loom in the sky. She created thousands of tiny lights, each one a knot of shimmering thread, and scattered them across the heavens.

As dawn approached, the Moon Goddess was delighted. "Your gift is magnificent! But what payment do you ask?"

Aria smiled. "Only this: when people look up at night, let them remember that even in darkness, beauty can be woven. Let each star remind them that small lights, together, can illuminate the world."

The goddess agreed, and from that night forward, the stars have sparkled above. And on clear nights, some say you can still see Aria among them, weaving new constellations for those who dare to dream.

